      "file": "deploy/build.py",
      "line": 116,
      "title": "Enforce the lockfile byte limit before reading it into root memory",
      "trigger": "An administrator installs or updates from a candidate source archive containing an oversized pnpm-lock.yaml. The source archive permits a regular member up to 512 MiB; dependency_input is called by Builder.prepare before entering the bounded isolated fetch/build units.",
      "impact": "Path.read_text reads the complete candidate lock and lock.encode creates another full-size allocation before the 8 MiB check rejects it. A permitted 512 MiB archive member can therefore cause approximately 1 GiB of root-process allocation before rejection. The direct root install/update preparation path has no memory cgroup bound here, so a malformed candidate can create host memory pressure or OOM disruption while the existing deployment is still running.",
      "evidence": [
        "deploy/build.py:116 reads the complete file; line 117 evaluates len(lock.encode()) before rejecting sizes above 8 MiB.",
        "deploy/safe_fs.py:19 permits 512 MiB per extracted file; deploy/build.py:232-235 extracts and calls dependency_input before isolated stages.",
        "deploy/cli.py:163-170 and 195-199 invoke install/update preparation directly as root; the 512 MiB transient manager unit applies to RPC operations, not this source-build path.",
